		{
			"id":"c5553f842d85ec32",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"HOMEWORK 1: E03 - APPLYING EXERCISE: PAGE 9\nHOMEWORK 2: TUCHON 1 WRITING TEST -> REPORT LAI\nHOMEWORK 3: TELL ME ABOUT ONE SPECIAL MOMENT OF YOUR SATURDAY 10.10.2026 - 3->5 SENTENES",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":4160,
			"y":4520,
			"width":500,
			"height":100,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"a427957b69901ecb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# IELTS SPEAKING MASTER GUIDE\n\n## Tất cả dạng câu hỏi + Expression Bank + Sentence Structures\n\nMục tiêu của guide này là giúp học viên **không học thuộc sample answer**, mà nhận diện loại câu hỏi và lập tức chọn đúng **answer structure + expressions**.\n\nCông thức tổng quát:\n\n> **Part 1 = Answer + Develop**  \n> **Part 2 = Tell a coherent story**  \n> **Part 3 = Analyse + Explain**\n\n---\n\n# PART 1 — PERSONAL INTERVIEW\n\n## 1. Part 1 cần làm gì?\n\nPart 1 thường hỏi về:\n\n- yourself\n- work / study\n- hometown\n- accommodation\n- hobbies\n- music\n- food\n- technology\n- transport\n- shopping\n- weekends\n- friends\n- weather\n- daily habits\n\nMỗi câu nên khoảng **2–4 sentences / 15–25 seconds**.\n\nCông thức mặc định:\n\n> **Direct Answer → Reason → Detail / Example**\n\nVí dụ:\n\n**Do you like cooking?**\n\n> Yes, quite a lot actually. I find cooking quite relaxing, especially after a long day at work. I usually make simple Vietnamese dishes because they're quick and easy to prepare.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 1 — LIKE / DISLIKE\n\n### Questions\n\n- Do you like...?\n- Do you enjoy...?\n- What's your favourite...?\n- What do you like about...?\n- Is there anything you dislike about...?\n\n### Structure\n\n> **Preference → Reason → Example/detail**\n\n### Expressions\n\n**Like**\n\n- I'm really into...\n- I'm quite fond of...\n- I'm a big fan of...\n- I have a soft spot for...\n- I'm particularly interested in...\n- I've always enjoyed...\n\n**Neutral**\n\n- I don't mind...\n- I'm fairly indifferent to...\n- It's not something I think much about.\n\n**Dislike**\n\n- I'm not particularly keen on...\n- It's not really my thing.\n- I'm not a huge fan of...\n- I tend to avoid...\n\n### Sentence structures\n\n> I'm particularly fond of **N/V-ing**, mainly because **S + V**.\n\n> What I like most about **N** is **N / the fact that + clause**.\n\n> I'm not particularly keen on **N**, mostly because **S + V**.\n\n### Example\n\n**Do you like crowded places?**\n\n> Not particularly. I tend to prefer quieter places because I find large crowds quite overwhelming. If I have the choice, I'd rather spend time in a small café than a busy shopping centre.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 2 — FREQUENCY / HABIT\n\n### Questions\n\n- How often do you...?\n- Do you often...?\n- When do you usually...?\n- How much time do you spend...?\n\n### Structure\n\n> **Frequency → Typical situation → Reason**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- pretty much every day\n- almost every day\n- once or twice a week\n- every now and then\n- once in a while\n- whenever I get the chance\n- on a regular basis\n- from time to time\n- hardly ever\n- very rarely\n\n### Sentence structures\n\n> I tend to **V** whenever **S + V**.\n\n> I usually **V**, especially when...\n\n> It depends on..., but generally...\n\n> On weekdays, I tend to..., whereas at weekends...\n\n### Example\n\n**How often do you listen to music?**\n\n> Pretty much every day. I normally listen to music while commuting or working out. It helps me switch off and makes repetitive activities a bit more enjoyable.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 3 — WHAT KIND / TYPE\n\n### Questions\n\n- What kind of music do you like?\n- What sort of books do you read?\n- What types of films do you watch?\n\n### Structure\n\n> **Main preference → Secondary preference → Example**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- I'm mostly into...\n- I tend to gravitate towards...\n- I usually go for...\n- I have quite a broad taste in...\n- It depends on my mood.\n- I'm open to...\n\n### Sentence structures\n\n> I'm mostly into **X**, although I occasionally enjoy **Y** as well.\n\n> I tend to gravitate towards **X**, particularly **specific example**.\n\n> My taste is fairly varied, but if I had to choose, I'd probably go for...\n\n### Example\n\n> I'm mostly into electronic music, particularly techno and house, although I listen to hip-hop from time to time as well. It really depends on my mood and what I'm doing.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 4 — WHY\n\n### Questions\n\n- Why do you like...?\n- Why is ... popular?\n- Why do you prefer...?\n\n### Structure\n\n> **Main reason → Explanation → Personal result**\n\nAvoid:\n\n> Because it's interesting.\n\nUse:\n\n> One of the main reasons is that...\n\n### Expressions\n\n- Mainly because...\n- One of the main reasons is...\n- I suppose it's because...\n- What appeals to me is...\n- The main attraction for me is...\n- A big part of it is...\n\n### Sentence structures\n\n> One of the main reasons I **V** is that...\n\n> What appeals to me most is the fact that...\n\n> A big part of the reason is that...\n\n### Example\n\n**Why do you enjoy travelling?**\n\n> One of the main reasons is that it gives me a change of scenery. I spend quite a lot of time working indoors, so travelling gives me a chance to disconnect from my normal routine.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 5 — PAST\n\n### Questions\n\n- Did you... as a child?\n- What did you do when you were younger?\n- Did you enjoy... in the past?\n- Has this changed since you were younger?\n\n### Must-use grammar\n\n- used to\n- would often\n- past simple\n- when I was younger\n- growing up\n\n### Expressions\n\n- When I was younger,...\n- Back when I was in school,...\n- Growing up,...\n- I used to...\n- I would often...\n- I wasn't really into... back then.\n\n### Structures\n\n> I used to **V**, but nowadays I...\n\n> When I was younger, I would often **V**.\n\n> I wasn't particularly interested in **N** back then, but...\n\n### Example\n\n**Did you read much as a child?**\n\n> Yes, quite a lot actually. I used to read comics almost every evening after school. These days, I mostly read articles and non-fiction instead.\n\nNotice:\n\n> **Past → Present contrast**\n\nrất hữu dụng.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 6 — CHANGE\n\n### Questions\n\n- Has your hometown changed?\n- Has your taste in music changed?\n- Are your hobbies different now?\n\n### Structure\n\n> **Before → Change → Now**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- It has changed quite a bit.\n- There has definitely been a shift.\n- Compared with a few years ago,...\n- I used to..., whereas now...\n- Over time, I've become more...\n\n### Structure\n\n> I used to **X**, whereas these days I tend to **Y**.\n\n> Compared with a few years ago, I'm much more...\n\n> My preferences have changed gradually over time.\n\n### Example\n\n> My taste in music has changed quite a bit. I used to listen almost exclusively to pop music, whereas these days I'm much more interested in techno and electronic music.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 7 — PREFERENCE / CHOICE\n\n### Questions\n\n- Do you prefer X or Y?\n- Would you rather...?\n- Which do you prefer?\n\n### Structure\n\n> **Choice → Reason → Contrast**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- I'd definitely go for...\n- Given the choice, I'd choose...\n- I tend to prefer...\n- I'd much rather...\n- Both have their advantages, but...\n\n### Sentence structure\n\n> Given the choice, I'd probably choose **X**, mainly because...\n\n> While **Y** can be..., I personally prefer **X** because...\n\n### Example\n\n> Given the choice, I'd definitely prefer working from home. I find it easier to concentrate in a quiet environment, although I do think office work is better for collaboration.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 8 — FUTURE / WOULD YOU LIKE TO\n\n### Questions\n\n- Would you like to...?\n- Do you think you will...?\n- Is there something you'd like to learn?\n- Where would you like to live in the future?\n\n### Structure\n\n> **Future intention → Reason → Condition**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- I'd definitely like to...\n- I'd love to...\n- I'd consider...\n- Hopefully,...\n- If I had the opportunity,...\n- At some point, I'd like to...\n\n### Structures\n\n> If I had the opportunity, I'd probably...\n\n> At some point in the future, I'd like to...\n\n> It's something I'd definitely consider if...\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 9 — IMPORTANCE\n\n### Questions\n\n- Is music important to you?\n- Is it important to exercise?\n- How important is...?\n\n### Structure\n\n> **Degree → Reason → Impact**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- It's quite important to me.\n- It plays a fairly important role in...\n- I wouldn't say it's essential, but...\n- It's become an important part of...\n- I probably couldn't do without...\n\n### Structure\n\n> It plays quite an important role in my life because...\n\n> I wouldn't say it's absolutely essential, but...\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 10 — DIFFICULT / EASY\n\n### Questions\n\n- Is it difficult to...?\n- Was it easy to learn...?\n- What is challenging about...?\n\n### Expressions\n\n- The hardest part is...\n- It can be quite challenging to...\n- It takes a while to...\n- There's definitely a learning curve.\n- Once you get used to it,...\n\n### Structure\n\n> The hardest part is **N/V-ing**, because...\n\n> It can be quite challenging at first, but once you...\n\n---\n\n# PART 1 UNIVERSAL EXPRESSION BANK\n\n## Starting naturally\n\n- Definitely.\n- For the most part, yes.\n- Not particularly.\n- I'd say so.\n- It depends, really.\n- Probably not.\n- Quite a lot, actually.\n\n## Giving reasons\n\n- Mainly because...\n- One reason is that...\n- I suppose it's because...\n- What I like about it is...\n- The main thing for me is...\n\n## Adding details\n\n- For example,...\n- Particularly...\n- Especially when...\n- Most of the time,...\n- In my case,...\n\n## Contrasting\n\n- That said,...\n- Having said that,...\n- Although...\n- Whereas...\n- On the other hand,...\n\n---\n\n# PART 2 — LONG TURN / CUE CARD\n\nPart 2 không nên học theo từng câu hỏi.\n\nHãy học theo **story functions**.\n\nUniversal structure:\n\n> **Context → Description → Development → Key Moment → Reaction → Meaning**\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 1 — DESCRIBE A PERSON\n\nCó thể hỏi:\n\n- someone you admire\n- a friend\n- a teacher\n- someone successful\n- someone interesting\n- a family member\n- someone who helped you\n\n## Structure\n\n### 1. Who\n\n> I'd like to talk about...\n\n### 2. How you know them\n\n> I first met him/her when...\n\n### 3. Personality / qualities\n\n> What really stands out about him/her is...\n\n### 4. Specific moment\n\n> One particular moment I remember clearly was...\n\n### 5. Why significant\n\n> That's probably why...\n\n## Expressions\n\n### Introducing\n\n- I'd like to talk about someone who...\n- The person who immediately comes to mind is...\n- Someone I've always looked up to is...\n\n### Personality\n\n- easy-going\n- down-to-earth\n- open-minded\n- reliable\n- quick-witted\n- thoughtful\n- genuinely kind\n- incredibly patient\n- someone you can rely on\n\n### Relationship\n\n- we hit it off immediately\n- we gradually became close\n- we've kept in touch ever since\n- I've known him/her for...\n- he/she has always been there for me\n\n### Reflection\n\n> What I appreciate most about him/her is...\n\n> Looking back, I think...\n\n> That's probably why he/she has had such a strong influence on me.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 2 — DESCRIBE A PLACE\n\nPossible topics:\n\n- city\n- natural place\n- restaurant\n- shop\n- building\n- home\n- tourist attraction\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Where → First impression → Features → Activity → Feeling**\n\n### Expressions\n\nLocation:\n\n- It's located in...\n- It's tucked away in...\n- It's right in the heart of...\n- It's surrounded by...\n\nAtmosphere:\n\n- peaceful and laid-back\n- lively and vibrant\n- cosy\n- spacious\n- slightly chaotic\n- breathtaking\n\nUseful structures:\n\n> What struck me first was...\n\n> One thing that makes the place special is...\n\n> What I particularly like about it is...\n\n> Whenever I'm there, I feel...\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 3 — DESCRIBE AN OBJECT\n\nPossible:\n\n- gift\n- photograph\n- piece of technology\n- clothing\n- book\n- useful item\n\n## Structure\n\n> **What → Where from → Appearance/function → How you use it → Meaning**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- I've had it for...\n- It was given to me by...\n- I use it mainly for...\n- It comes in handy when...\n- I've grown quite attached to it.\n- It has sentimental value.\n- It's nothing particularly expensive, but...\n\nExcellent frame:\n\n> It's not particularly valuable in monetary terms, but it means a lot to me because...\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 4 — DESCRIBE AN EXPERIENCE / EVENT\n\nPossible:\n\n- memorable trip\n- celebration\n- achievement\n- difficult experience\n- exciting activity\n- competition\n- concert\n\n## Best structure\n\n> **Before → During → Turning Point → After → Reflection**\n\n### Expressions\n\nSetting:\n\n- It happened...\n- If I remember correctly,...\n- At the time,...\n- I wasn't really sure what to expect.\n\nDevelopment:\n\n- At first,...\n- A little while later,...\n- Eventually,...\n- Out of nowhere,...\n- To my surprise,...\n\nReaction:\n\n- I was absolutely thrilled.\n- I was relieved.\n- I felt slightly overwhelmed.\n- I couldn't believe it.\n- It completely caught me off guard.\n\nReflection:\n\n- Looking back,...\n- In hindsight,...\n- What made it particularly memorable was...\n- It's something I'll probably remember for a long time.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 5 — DESCRIBE AN ACTIVITY / SKILL\n\nPossible:\n\n- hobby\n- sport\n- activity\n- skill you learned\n- something difficult\n- something you want to learn\n\n## Structure\n\n> **What → How started → Learning process → Challenge → Benefit**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- I first got into it when...\n- I picked it up from...\n- It took me a while to...\n- The hardest part was...\n- I gradually got better at...\n- Once I got the hang of it,...\n- It gives me a sense of...\n\n### Structures\n\n> It took me quite a while to get used to **V-ing**.\n\n> The more I practised, the easier it became.\n\nVery useful:\n\n> **The more + S + V, the more + S + V.**\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 6 — DESCRIBE SOMETHING YOU WANT\n\nPossible:\n\n- job\n- house\n- skill\n- place to visit\n- product\n- future goal\n\n## Structure\n\n> **What → Why → Benefit → Challenge → Future**\n\n### Useful structures\n\n> Something I'd really like to do is...\n\n> If I had the chance, I'd...\n\n> The main reason I'm interested in it is...\n\n> Hopefully, I'll be able to...\n\n---\n\n# PART 2 UNIVERSAL STORY TOOLKIT\n\n## Setting the scene\n\n- It happened a couple of years ago.\n- If I remember correctly,...\n- I was still studying at the time.\n- It was during...\n- I first came across...\n\n## Building the story\n\n- At first,...\n- Not long after that,...\n- A little later,...\n- Eventually,...\n- Before I knew it,...\n\n## Highlighting something\n\n- What stands out most is...\n- What I remember most clearly is...\n- The thing that really stuck with me was...\n- One particular moment stands out.\n\n## Feelings\n\nInstead of:\n\n> very happy\n\nUse:\n\n- thrilled\n- delighted\n- relieved\n- pleasantly surprised\n\nInstead of:\n\n> very nervous\n\nUse:\n\n- anxious\n- slightly overwhelmed\n- on edge\n- under pressure\n\n## Ending\n\nUse:\n\n> Looking back,...\n\n> In hindsight,...\n\n> What made it special wasn't X, but Y.\n\nExcellent:\n\n> Looking back, what made the experience special wasn't really what happened, but the people I shared it with.\n\n---\n\n# PART 3 — ABSTRACT DISCUSSION\n\nĐây là part học viên cần nâng cấp mạnh nhất.\n\nKhông còn:\n\n> I like...\n\nMà chuyển sang:\n\n> People tend to...  \n> One possible explanation is...  \n> This may be because...\n\nUniversal structure:\n\n> **Position → Reason → Explain → Example → Impact / Qualification**\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 1 — WHY / CAUSES\n\n### Questions\n\n- Why do people...?\n- Why has X become popular?\n- What causes...?\n- Why is X becoming more common?\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Cause → Mechanism → Result**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- One major reason is...\n- A significant factor is...\n- This largely comes down to...\n- One possible explanation is...\n- Another contributing factor is...\n- This may partly be because...\n\n### Sentence structures\n\n> One major reason **why X happens** is that...\n\n> This largely comes down to the fact that...\n\n> As **X increases**, people are more likely to...\n\n### Example\n\n**Why do people shop online?**\n\n> One major reason is convenience. Online shopping allows consumers to compare products and make purchases without travelling to a physical store. This is particularly attractive to people with busy schedules.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 2 — EFFECTS / CONSEQUENCES\n\n### Questions\n\n- What effects does... have?\n- How does... affect people?\n- What are the consequences?\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Change → Immediate effect → Wider consequence**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- This can lead to...\n- This tends to result in...\n- One consequence is...\n- In the long run,...\n- This can have a significant impact on...\n- A knock-on effect is...\n\n### Structures\n\n> This can lead to **N**, which in turn can...\n\n> One immediate consequence is..., while in the longer term...\n\nExcellent chain:\n\n> **X → leads to Y → which means Z**\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 3 — ADVANTAGES / DISADVANTAGES\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Advantage → Why → Example → Disadvantage → Why**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- One clear advantage is...\n- One major benefit is...\n- The obvious downside is...\n- The main drawback is...\n- There is also the risk that...\n\n### Structure\n\n> One clear benefit of **X** is that...\n\n> The downside, however, is that...\n\n> While **X** offers..., it may also...\n\nAvoid listing:\n\n> Firstly... secondly... thirdly...\n\nDevelop fewer ideas better.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 4 — AGREE / DISAGREE\n\n### Questions\n\n- Do you agree...?\n- Do you think this is a good idea?\n- Should people...?\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Position → Reason → Counterpoint → Final judgement**\n\n### Expressions\n\nStrong agreement:\n\n- Yes, absolutely.\n- For the most part, yes.\n- I strongly believe that...\n\nPartial:\n\n- To some extent, yes.\n- I agree up to a point.\n- There is some truth in that, but...\n- It depends largely on...\n\nDisagreement:\n\n- Not necessarily.\n- I'm not entirely convinced.\n- I wouldn't go that far.\n\nVery useful Band 7 structure:\n\n> While I can understand why some people think **X**, I would argue that **Y**.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 5 — COMPARE PAST AND PRESENT\n\n### Questions\n\n- How has X changed?\n- Is X different nowadays?\n- How was X different in the past?\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Past → Driver of change → Present → Consequence**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- Compared with the past,...\n- Traditionally,...\n- In previous generations,...\n- These days,...\n- There has been a noticeable shift towards...\n- X has become increasingly...\n\n### Structures\n\n> Whereas people used to **X**, nowadays they tend to **Y**.\n\n> There has been a gradual shift from **X** towards **Y**.\n\n> Compared with previous generations, people today are much more likely to...\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 6 — FUTURE / PREDICTION\n\n### Questions\n\n- How will X change?\n- What might happen in the future?\n- Do you think X will become more common?\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Prediction → Driver → Result → Limitation**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- I imagine...\n- I suspect...\n- X is likely to...\n- We may see...\n- There's a good chance that...\n- I wouldn't be surprised if...\n- It's difficult to predict, but...\n\n### Structures\n\n> As technology continues to develop, **X is likely to...**\n\n> If current trends continue, we may see...\n\n> I wouldn't be surprised if **S + past verb**...\n\nExample:\n\n> I wouldn't be surprised if remote work became even more common.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 7 — PROBLEM / SOLUTION\n\n### Questions\n\n- What can be done about...?\n- How can governments solve...?\n- What should people do?\n- How could this problem be reduced?\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Problem → Intervention → Mechanism → Expected result**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- One effective approach would be to...\n- Governments could...\n- A practical solution would be...\n- Another option is...\n- Rather than simply..., authorities could...\n\n### Structures\n\n> One effective way to address this issue would be to...\n\n> If governments **past tense**, people would be more likely to...\n\n> This could help to reduce...\n\nExample:\n\n> One effective approach would be to improve public transport. If buses and metro systems were reliable and affordable, commuters would be less dependent on private vehicles.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 8 — IMPORTANCE\n\n### Questions\n\n- How important is...?\n- Why is X important?\n- Is X more important now?\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Judgement → Function → Consequence**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- It plays a crucial role in...\n- It's particularly important because...\n- Its importance shouldn't be underestimated.\n- I wouldn't necessarily say it's essential, but...\n\n### Structures\n\n> X plays an important role in **V-ing** because...\n\n> Without X, it would be much harder for people to...\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 9 — WHO IS RESPONSIBLE?\n\n### Questions\n\n- Should parents or schools...?\n- Should governments or individuals...?\n- Who should be responsible for...?\n\n## Best strategy\n\nDon't automatically choose one side.\n\nUse **shared responsibility** when logical.\n\n### Expressions\n\n- Responsibility should probably be shared.\n- X has the primary responsibility for...\n- Y also has a role to play.\n- Ultimately,...\n- At an individual level,...\n- At an institutional level,...\n\n### Structure\n\n> X should be responsible for **A**, whereas Y should focus on **B**.\n\nExample:\n\n> Parents probably have the greatest influence on children's daily habits, whereas schools are better placed to provide structured education.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 10 — WHICH IS BETTER?\n\n### Questions\n\n- Is X better than Y?\n- Which is more important?\n- Are traditional methods better?\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Criteria → X strength → Y strength → Verdict**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- It depends on what you mean by...\n- If the priority is X,...\n- In terms of...\n- X tends to be better for..., whereas Y is more suitable for...\n- There's no one-size-fits-all answer.\n\nExcellent:\n\n> I don't think one is necessarily better than the other; they serve different purposes.\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 11 — DIFFERENT GROUPS\n\n### Questions\n\n- Do young and old people think differently?\n- Are men and women interested in different things?\n- Do people in cities behave differently from rural residents?\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Group A → Group B → Reason → Qualification**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- Younger people tend to...\n- Older generations are generally more likely to...\n- This difference may partly be due to...\n- Of course, this is a generalisation.\n\n### Useful structure\n\n> While younger people tend to **X**, older generations may be more inclined to **Y**.\n\nImportant:\n\nAvoid absolute claims:\n\n> All young people...\n\nBetter:\n\n> Younger people **tend to / are more likely to**...\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 12 — SHOULD / POLICY\n\n### Questions\n\n- Should governments ban...?\n- Should companies provide...?\n- Should schools teach...?\n\n## Structure\n\n> **Position → Practical benefit → Risk → Condition**\n\n### Expressions\n\n- In principle, yes.\n- I think it would make sense to...\n- A blanket ban may not be necessary.\n- Regulation might be more effective than prohibition.\n- It would depend on how the policy was implemented.\n\nVery strong:\n\n> Rather than banning X altogether, it might be more practical to regulate...\n\n---\n\n# TYPE 13 — SOCIAL TREND\n\n### Questions\n\n- Why are people marrying later?\n- Why do people change jobs more often?\n- Why do people spend more time online?\n\nUse multi-cause thinking:\n\n> **Technology + Economy + Lifestyle + Culture**\n\n### Framework\n\n> I think several factors are involved. One is..., while another is...\n\nUseful:\n\n> There probably isn't a single explanation.\n\n> It's likely to be a combination of...\n\n> Economic factors also play a role.\n\n---\n\n# PART 3 HIGH-VALUE LOGIC STRUCTURES\n\n## 1. Cause\n\n> This is largely because...\n\n> One explanation for this is...\n\n## 2. Result\n\n> As a result,...\n\n> Consequently,...\n\n> This means that...\n\n> This can lead to...\n\n## 3. Contrast\n\n> While...\n\n> Whereas...\n\n> However,...\n\n> That said,...\n\n## 4. Concession\n\n> Although X may..., it can also...\n\n> While this is certainly true to some extent,...\n\n## 5. Condition\n\n> If governments invested more in..., people would...\n\n> Unless..., it is unlikely that...\n\n## 6. Relative clause\n\n> People who live in large cities often...\n\n> Technology, which has become increasingly accessible, allows...\n\n## 7. Comparative\n\n> X is considerably more common than it used to be.\n\n> People are far more likely to...\n\n## 8. Trend\n\n> There has been a growing tendency for people to...\n\n> X is becoming increasingly common.\n\n---\n\n# THE “WHY → HOW → SO WHAT?” METHOD\n\nĐây là technique tốt nhất cho Part 3.\n\nQuestion:\n\n> Why are people becoming dependent on technology?\n\n### WHY?\n\nBecause it is convenient.\n\n### HOW?\n\nIt allows people to complete tasks instantly.\n\n### SO WHAT?\n\nThey gradually stop using traditional alternatives.\n\nComplete answer:\n\n> One major reason is convenience. Technology allows people to complete everyday tasks almost instantly, from communicating to banking and shopping. As a result, people gradually become accustomed to using digital tools and may find traditional alternatives unnecessarily slow or inconvenient.\n\nĐây là khác biệt giữa:\n\n> **answering**\n\nvà\n\n> **developing an argument.**\n\n---\n\n# EXPRESSION BANK: BUYING THINKING TIME\n\nĐừng:\n\n> Umm... err... you know... like...\n\nUse:\n\n- That's an interesting question.\n- I've never really thought about that before.\n- Off the top of my head,...\n- I'd probably say...\n- I suppose...\n- It really depends.\n- There are probably several reasons.\n\nChỉ dùng **1 phrase**, rồi trả lời.\n\n---\n\n# EXPRESSIONS FOR EXAMPLES\n\nBasic:\n\n> For example,...\n\nBetter:\n\n> A good example would be...\n\n> Take X, for instance.\n\n> This is particularly obvious in...\n\n> To give you an example,...\n\nPart 3:\n\n> Take large cities such as Ho Chi Minh City, for instance.\n\n---\n\n# EXPRESSIONS FOR NUANCE\n\nBand 7+ rất cần.\n\nInstead of:\n\n> Yes.\n\nUse:\n\n- To some extent.\n- For the most part.\n- In many cases.\n- Not necessarily.\n- It largely depends on...\n- Generally speaking,...\n- In certain situations,...\n- That's probably true for..., but...\n\n---\n\n# EXPRESSIONS TO AVOID OVERCLAIMING\n\nBad:\n\n> Technology destroys relationships.\n\nBetter:\n\n> Technology **can sometimes weaken** face-to-face interaction.\n\nUse:\n\n- can\n- may\n- might\n- tends to\n- is likely to\n- in some cases\n- generally\n\nAcademic reasoning tốt hơn nhiều.\n\n---\n\n# UNIVERSAL SENTENCE FRAMES — PART 1\n\n### Personal preference\n\n> I'm quite fond of **N/V-ing**, mainly because...\n\n### Habit\n\n> I tend to **V** whenever...\n\n### Past\n\n> I used to **V**, whereas nowadays...\n\n### Change\n\n> Over the years, I've become more...\n\n### Future\n\n> If I had the opportunity, I'd probably...\n\n### Reason\n\n> One of the main reasons I **V** is that...\n\n### Contrast\n\n> Although I enjoy **X**, I tend to prefer **Y**.\n\n---\n\n# UNIVERSAL SENTENCE FRAMES — PART 2\n\n### Introduction\n\n> I'd like to talk about...\n\n### Background\n\n> I first came across / met / visited it when...\n\n### Description\n\n> What really stands out about it is...\n\n### Story\n\n> One particular moment I remember clearly was when...\n\n### Reaction\n\n> I remember feeling...\n\n### Reflection\n\n> Looking back, what made it memorable was...\n\n---\n\n# UNIVERSAL SENTENCE FRAMES — PART 3\n\n### Opinion\n\n> From my perspective,...\n\n### Cause\n\n> One major reason for this is that...\n\n### Explanation\n\n> What this means in practice is that...\n\n### Effect\n\n> This can lead to...\n\n### Example\n\n> A good example would be...\n\n### Contrast\n\n> While X may..., Y...\n\n### Qualification\n\n> That said, this isn't necessarily true in every case.\n\n### Future\n\n> If current trends continue,...\n\n### Solution\n\n> One effective way to address this would be to...\n\n---\n\n# Speaking Response Builder\n\nMột học viên có thể học hệ thống này thay vì học 100 sample answers.\n\n## PART 1\n\n> **Answer**\n> \n> I tend to...\n> \n> **Why**\n> \n> mainly because...\n> \n> **Detail**\n> \n> especially when...\n\nExample:\n\n> I tend to exercise in the evening, mainly because that's when I'm free from work. I usually go for a short run or work out at home.\n\n---\n\n# PART 2\n\n> **Context**\n> \n> I'd like to talk about...\n> \n> **Background**\n> \n> I first...\n> \n> **Development**\n> \n> What really stands out...\n> \n> **Moment**\n> \n> One moment I remember clearly...\n> \n> **Meaning**\n> \n> Looking back...\n\n---\n\n# PART 3\n\n> **Answer**\n> \n> I think...\n> \n> **Why**\n> \n> One major reason is...\n> \n> **How**\n> \n> This means that...\n> \n> **Example**\n> \n> For example,...\n> \n> **So what**\n> \n> As a result,...\n> \n> **Balance**\n> \n> That said,...\n\n---\n\n# Final Cheat Sheet\n\n|Part|Student needs to do|Core structure|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Part 1**|Talk naturally about yourself|**Answer → Reason → Detail**|\n|**Part 2**|Tell one coherent story|**Context → Description → Moment → Meaning**|\n|**Part 3**|Analyse an issue|**Position → Why → How → Example → So What**|\n\n### PART 1 THINK\n\n> **ME**\n\nMy experience  \nMy habits  \nMy preferences  \nMy past/future\n\n### PART 2 THINK\n\n> **STORY**\n\nWho/what  \nContext  \nWhat happened  \nReaction  \nMeaning\n\n### PART 3 THINK\n\n> **LOGIC**\n\nWhy?  \nHow?  \nExample?  \nResult?  \nOther side?\n\nNếu phải dạy học viên **chỉ một nguyên tắc cho mỗi Part**, tôi sẽ chốt:\n\n> **Part 1:** Don't just answer. **Add one layer.**  \n> **Part 2:** Don't answer four bullets. **Tell one story.**  \n> **Part 3:** Don't just give an opinion. **Explain the logic behind it.**\n\n### Prompt cải thiện\n\n> **Create a complete IELTS Speaking master worksheet in Vietnamese covering all major question types in Parts 1, 2, and 3. For each question type, include: what the examiner is testing, a step-by-step answer structure, reusable natural expressions, Band 7+ sentence frameworks, common mistakes, and 1–2 short model answers. Prioritise highly reusable language over topic-specific vocabulary. Clearly distinguish Part 1 personal answers, Part 2 storytelling, and Part 3 analytical reasoning. Make the material practical for Vietnamese learners and suitable for classroom use.**",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9440,
			"y":-1650,
			"width":940,
			"height":39567
		},
		{
			"id":"494a8d04db9a4920",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# IELTS INTENSIVE 26.3 — 8-SESSION HOMEWORK SYSTEM\n\n8 weeks · 3 homework tasks per session · Writing + Speaking + Personal Reflection\n\nThe system follows your original homework format, with increasing difficulty across eight sessions. Each week combines knowledge application, IELTS Writing Task 2 practice, and natural English expression.\n\n## 1. Homework structure\n\n|Homework|Purpose|Expected output|\n|---|---|---|\n|HW1 — Applying Exercise|Reinforce concepts taught in class|Complete the assigned worksheet exercises|\n|HW2 — IELTS Writing Task 2|Practice question analysis, argument development and essay writing|One Task 2 response + short analysis|\n|HW3 — Personal Expression|Develop active vocabulary, storytelling and fluency|3–5 natural English sentences|\n\n## 2. Eight-session homework plan\n\n## Session 1 — Writing Foundations\n\n10 Oct 2026\n\nFOCUS: TASK ANALYSIS & IDEA DEVELOPMENT\n\nHOMEWORK 1 — APPLYING EXERCISE\n\nComplete E03 — Applying Exercise, Page 9.\n\nHOMEWORK 2 — IELTS WRITING TASK 2\n\nChoose ONE IELTS Writing Task 2 question from the workbook. Write a complete essay and submit it for feedback.\n\nHOMEWORK 3 — PERSONAL EXPRESSION\n\nTell me about one special moment of your Saturday, October 10, 2026.\n\nWrite 3–5 sentences describing what happened, how you felt, and why the moment was special.\n\n## Session 2 — Opinions & Arguments\n\n17 Oct 2026\n\nFOCUS: AGREE / DISAGREE\n\nHOMEWORK 1 — APPLYING EXERCISE\n\nPractice turning an idea into an argument using Claim → Why → How → Example → Result. Develop TWO arguments from the session worksheet.\n\nHOMEWORK 2 — IELTS WRITING TASK 2\n\nSome people believe that students should focus on academic subjects, while others think practical life skills are equally important. To what extent do you agree or disagree?\n\nWrite at least 250 words. Clearly state and defend your position.\n\nHOMEWORK 3 — PERSONAL EXPRESSION\n\nTell me about something you strongly believe in.\n\nWrite 3–5 sentences expressing your opinion and explaining why you believe it.\n\n## Session 3 — Different Perspectives\n\n24 Oct 2026\n\nFOCUS: DISCUSS BOTH VIEWS + OPINION\n\nHOMEWORK 1 — APPLYING EXERCISE\n\nAnalyze two opposing viewpoints. Complete the session worksheet by writing one claim and one supporting explanation for each viewpoint.\n\nHOMEWORK 2 — IELTS WRITING TASK 2\n\nSome people believe that online learning is more effective than traditional classroom learning, while others disagree. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.\n\nWrite at least 250 words. Develop both perspectives and make your own position clear.\n\nHOMEWORK 3 — PERSONAL EXPRESSION\n\nTell me about a time when you disagreed with someone.\n\nIn 3–5 sentences, explain what you disagreed about, how you responded, and what happened afterward.\n\n## Session 4 — Evaluating Advantages & Disadvantages\n\n31 Oct 2026\n\nFOCUS: EVALUATION & COMPARISON\n\nHOMEWORK 1 — APPLYING EXERCISE\n\nIdentify advantages and disadvantages, then explain which side carries more weight using examples and consequences.\n\nHOMEWORK 2 — IELTS WRITING TASK 2\n\nAn increasing number of people prefer working from home rather than travelling to an office. Do the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?\n\nWrite at least 250 words. Compare both sides and justify your evaluation.\n\nHOMEWORK 3 — PERSONAL EXPRESSION\n\nTell me about an important decision you made recently.\n\nWrite 3–5 sentences explaining your options, their advantages or disadvantages, and why you made that choice.\n\n## Session 5 — Problems & Solutions\n\n7 Nov 2026\n\nFOCUS: PROBLEM-SOLVING & LOGICAL REASONING\n\nHOMEWORK 1 — APPLYING EXERCISE\n\nIdentify a social problem, describe its consequences and develop one practical solution with an explanation of how it works.\n\nHOMEWORK 2 — IELTS WRITING TASK 2\n\nTraffic congestion has become a serious problem in many major cities. What problems does this cause, and what measures could be taken to address them?\n\nWrite at least 250 words. Connect the solutions directly to the problems you identify.\n\nHOMEWORK 3 — PERSONAL EXPRESSION\n\nTell me about a problem you faced this week and how you solved it.\n\nWrite 3–5 sentences describing the situation, your actions and the outcome.\n\n## Session 6 — Causes & Effects\n\n14 Nov 2026\n\nFOCUS: CAUSE-AND-EFFECT REASONING\n\nHOMEWORK 1 — APPLYING EXERCISE\n\nDevelop two cause-and-effect chains. Explain the underlying reasons, mechanisms and consequences.\n\nHOMEWORK 2 — IELTS WRITING TASK 2\n\nIn many countries, fewer people are reading books for pleasure. What are the causes and effects of this trend?\n\nWrite at least 250 words. Focus on explaining why this trend occurs and what consequences follow.\n\nHOMEWORK 3 — PERSONAL EXPRESSION\n\nTell me about a habit that has changed your life.\n\nWrite 3–5 sentences describing how the habit started and how it has affected you.\n\n## Session 7 — Positive & Negative Developments\n\n21 Nov 2026\n\nFOCUS: CRITICAL THINKING & EVALUATION\n\nHOMEWORK 1 — APPLYING EXERCISE\n\nEvaluate a social trend from different perspectives: individuals, families, education and society. Explain who benefits, who may be disadvantaged and why.\n\nHOMEWORK 2 — IELTS WRITING TASK 2\n\nMore people are using artificial intelligence tools in their studies and daily lives. Is this a positive or negative development?\n\nWrite at least 250 words. Present a clear overall judgment supported by logical arguments.\n\nHOMEWORK 3 — PERSONAL EXPRESSION\n\nTell me about a change in your life that initially seemed negative but eventually became positive.\n\nWrite 3–5 sentences describing the change and what you learned from it.\n\n## Session 8 — Final Integration & Reflection\n\n28 Nov 2026\n\nFOCUS: TWO-PART QUESTIONS & INDEPENDENT WRITING\n\nHOMEWORK 1 — APPLYING EXERCISE\n\nReview the question types covered in the course. For three different prompts, identify the task requirements, develop a thesis statement and create a two-paragraph essay outline.\n\nHOMEWORK 2 — IELTS WRITING TASK 2\n\nMany young people spend a significant amount of their free time on social media. Why is this happening? Do you think this is a positive or negative development?\n\nComplete a full essay of at least 250 words in 40 minutes, without AI-generated writing assistance.\n\nHOMEWORK 3 — PERSONAL REFLECTION\n\nTell me about how your English has changed over the past eight sessions.\n\nWrite 3–5 sentences describing what you can do better now, what you are proud of, and what you want to improve next.\n\nNote: Sessions 2–8 contain proposed original practice questions and exercise instructions. Specific E-codes and page numbers remain to be assigned from the corresponding worksheets.\n\n## 3. Weekly submission and feedback system\n\nWeekly homework tracker — Interactive template\n\nSession 1\n\n0/3 completed\n\nHW1 — Applying Exercise\n\nHW2 — IELTS Writing Task 2\n\nHW3 — Personal Expression\n\nCopy homework tracker\n\n### Submission standards\n\n|Item|Time guideline|Teacher checks|\n|---|---|---|\n|HW1|10–20 minutes|Understanding and application of frameworks|\n|HW2|40 minutes|Task Response, Coherence & Cohesion, Lexical Resource, Grammar|\n|HW3|5–10 minutes|Natural expression, clarity, vocabulary and sentence accuracy|\n\nFor HW2, students should additionally submit a short Task Analysis: question type, position/thesis, Body 1 main idea and Body 2 main idea. This helps you separate problems in reasoning from problems in English proficiency.\n\nFor HW3, an optional 60–90-second voice recording of the same reflection can connect Writing to Speaking without creating an entirely new assignment.\n\n### Reusable weekly feedback\n\nUse one short feedback record per student:\n\n- What worked: One clear strength.\n    \n- Biggest issue: One priority error or weakness.\n    \n- Improved expression: Two reusable sentences or collocations.\n    \n- Next target: One measurable action for the following week.\n    \n\nThis creates a consistent Assign → Submit → Diagnose → Correct → Reapply cycle across all eight sessions.\n\n## Improved prompt\n\nDesign a progressive 8-session homework system for my IELTS Intensive course, using three assignments per session:\n\n1. HW1 — Applying Exercise: Reinforce concepts from the current lesson using the assigned workbook exercises.\n    \n2. HW2 — IELTS Writing Task 2: Practice a specific essay type, progressing from question analysis and argument development to independent timed writing.\n    \n3. HW3 — Personal Expression: Write 3–5 natural English sentences about a real experience, opinion, decision, problem, or reflection.\n    \n\nKeep homework practical, manageable and increasingly challenging. Cover the major IELTS Writing Task 2 question types, include actual essay prompts, specify expected deliverables, and introduce reusable language skills.\n\nCreate a consistent submission format, evaluation criteria, progress tracker and weekly feedback loop. Make the instructions clear, natural, student-facing and ready to distribute. Do not invent workbook exercise codes or page numbers that have not been provided.\n\nAdd the eight session topics and datesMap each essay prompt to a question type",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1720,
			"y":-220,
			"width":960,
			"height":5940
